    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I am currently overseeing the acquisition of a new collection of rare 19th-century manuscripts for our university library here in Belgium. I am trying to decide whether we should implement the Dewey Decimal Classification (DDC) or the Library of Congress Classification (LCC) for this specific collection. Could you provide a comparative analysis of the two systems, specifically regarding their granularity for historical manuscripts and their scalability for growing archival collections?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Dewey Decimal Classification (DDC) system, developed by Melvil Dewey, is more widely used in North American libraries and is known for its simplicity and ease of use. The DDC is organized from 000 - General, through 900 - Philosophy, Religion, etc. The LCC, on the other hand, was developed by the library of Congress and is often seen as more detailed and complex, especially suited for collections with heavy geographical or period focus. For your collection of 19th-century manuscripts, the LCC might be more appropriate given its greater granularity for historical manuscripts, which would facilitate detailed categorization of content. Additionally, LCC's notation allows for greater scalability as your collection grows, as it would be easier to add new sub-categories specific to your collection without overhauling existing classifications.",
