    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. My main concern is that if we go with a system that's too rigid, any items that don't fit perfectly into a specific category might end up in a sort of \"catch-all\" or miscellaneous section. How do these two systems typically handle items that are highly irregular, fragmented, or simply don't follow a clean organizational logic? I'm worried about the chaos that might occur in the stacks if we don't have a way to account for those outliers.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Both the Dewey Decimal Classification and Library of Congress Classification systems provide provisions for items which don't neatly fit into standard categories. The LCC, being more detailed, has a comprehensive range of subsidiary tables that allow librarians to create customized notations for unusual items or subject areas. This personalized approach can reduce the edge cases you referred to in terms of \"catch-all\" sections, by allowing precise pinpointing of non-standard materials.\n\n\nOn the other hand, the DDC also recognizes the need for exceptions. It's common practice to add notation numbers from the tables to existing classifications to indicate topics that don't neatly fit. For instance, an unusual play by a 19th-century author might go from 776.9 to 776.94 in the DDC for \"Classical literature, plays and dramas\", which is intended for the subclass for \"Mixed and crossing subjects.\"\n\n\nDespite these mechanisms to accommodate irregular or mixed materials, it is crucial for librarians to have standard operating procedures (SOPs) in place for categorizing items that defy clear classification. Training and consultation from experienced librarians can mitigate potential disarray and maintain a comprehensible and orderly shelving system, whether you choose DDC, LCC, or even a modified hybrid system.",
      "source": "target",
